# `math::Function::sqrt`

[Back to `math::Function`](../Function.md)

The square root of a function, as a new function.

## Signature

```cpp
math::Function math::Function::sqrt() const;
```

## What It Does

`f.sqrt()` builds `x ↦ √f(x)`. For a real function the square root exists only for nonnegative values, and the function checks this on the values that arrive: a negative input raises `sqrt domain requires nonnegative values`. For a complex function it is the principal root, so `sqrt(−4 + 0i) = 2i`.

The derivative is `(√u)′ = u′ / (2√u)`, which is infinite at 0: the square root has a vertical tangent there. `√(x²)` is `|x|`, and the library simplifies `sqrt(x.pow(2))` to `abs(x)`; with [`assume`](assume.md) it becomes `x` or `−x`.

## Returns

A new `Function` whose value is `sqrt` of the receiver's value, with the same variables and output shape.

## Errors

- The function must be defined.
- A negative real input raises `sqrt domain requires nonnegative values`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

`sqrt` evaluated on a small grid, next to the standard-library value. Each element is computed independently, so the result has the shape of the input.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function f = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x.sqrt(); });
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({0.25, 1.0, 4.0, 9.0});
  out.writeln("x:           ", x.items<double>());
  out.writeln("f(x):        ", f({x}).items<double>());
  out.writeln("std::sqrt(x):", pluss::make_series({std::sqrt((0.25)), std::sqrt((1.0)), std::sqrt((4.0)), std::sqrt((9.0))}).items<double>());
}
```

The two rows agree to the printed digits.

### Example 2

The derivative rule. Differentiating `sqrt` with `operators::differential` gives the exact formula, `(√x)′ = 1/(2√x)`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function f = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x.sqrt(); });
  const math::Function df = operators::differential("x")(f);
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({0.25, 1.0, 4.0, 9.0});
  const auto exact = [](double v) { return 0.5 / std::sqrt(v); };
  out.writeln("f'(x):        ", df({x}).items<double>());
  out.writeln("closed form:  ", pluss::make_series({exact(0.25), exact(1.0), exact(4.0), exact(9.0)}).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3

The domain check, and the principal root of a complex input.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function f = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x.sqrt(); });
  try {
    (void)f(-4.0);
  } catch (const pluss::Error &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    out.writeln("sqrt(-4):       ", text.substr(0, text.find('\n')));
  }
  const std::complex<double> r = f(std::complex<double>{-4.0, 0.0}).item<std::complex<double>>();
  out.writeln("sqrt(-4 + 0i) = ", r.real(), "+", r.imag(), "i");
}
```

### Example 4

`sqrt(x²)` is `|x|`, not `x`. Without a declaration the function keeps the absolute value; declaring the sign with `assume` removes it.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function root = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x.pow(2).sqrt(); });
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({-3.0, 2.0});
  out.writeln("sqrt(x^2):               ", root({x}).items<double>());
  out.writeln("sqrt(x^2) | NonNegative: ", root.assume("x", math::Assumption::NonNegative)({x}).items<double>());
  out.writeln("sqrt(x^2) | NonPositive: ", root.assume("x", math::Assumption::NonPositive)({x}).items<double>());
}
```

The last two lines are promises (here false for one point each), not checks; see [`assume`](assume.md).

